; engine.pin.xon -- which engine this tree builds against, and where to get it.
;
; `make engine` reads this file, acquires what it names, and points the `engine`
; link at the result.  Everything downstream sees a directory and does not care
; how it arrived: a checkout is a superset of a release, which is the whole
; premise of the arrangement.
;
; DECLARATIVE, NOT EXECUTABLE, like pin.xon: a closed vocabulary of four forms,
; read by a shell script that runs BEFORE any engine exists and so cannot use x
; to parse it.  A form the script does not know is an error, never a shrug.
;
; FORMAT (rigid, one form per line -- the sed that reads this parses the same
; bytes):
;   (engine "NAME")                      which implementation
;   (release "TAG")                      which release of it
;   (artifact OS ARCH "sha256:HEX" "URL")  a prebuilt engine for one platform
;   (source "URL")                       where the sources are, for platforms
;                                        with no artifact row
;
; OS and ARCH use the CONTRACT'S spellings -- os is darwin/linux/bsd, arch is
; arm64/x86-64/i386 -- the same ones tools/contract/gen-build-params.sh writes
; into x-engine-build.xon.  Two vocabularies for one fact is how "aarch64"
; against "arm64" becomes a download that silently does not exist.
;
; SELECTION USES uname, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE IT IS RIGHT.  Everywhere else
; the platform comes from the engine's own declaration, because the compiler
; knows the target and uname knows only the host.  Here there is no engine yet
; and the question is "what will run on THIS machine", which is exactly what
; uname answers.  Cross-compiling is out of scope for a download: point
; X_ENGINE_DIR at an engine you built.

(engine "x-engine-c")

; --- WHERE THE SOURCES ARE ---------------------------------------------------
; Used for platforms this file declares no artifact for.  While the submodule is
; still here it is preferred over cloning: it is already at the commit this tree
; was tested against, and re-cloning to reach the same bytes would be theatre.
(source "https://github.com/jonruttan/x-engine-c.git")

; --- ARTIFACTS ---------------------------------------------------------------
; DELIBERATELY EMPTY.  x-engine-c's release pipeline landed before its first
; release did, so there is nothing yet to pin: every platform takes the source
; arm, which is what this tree already did.  The machinery is here, gated by
; tools/check/engine-fetch.sh against a local fixture, so that turning it on is
; adding rows rather than writing code.
;
; When v0.1.0 is cut, add its release row and one artifact row per published
; platform -- the digests are in the release's .sha256 sidecars:
;
;   (release "v0.1.0")
;   (artifact darwin arm64  "sha256:..." "https://github.com/jonruttan/x-engine-c/releases/download/v0.1.0/x-engine-c-v0.1.0-darwin-arm64.tar.gz")
;   (artifact linux  x86-64 "sha256:..." "https://github.com/jonruttan/x-engine-c/releases/download/v0.1.0/x-engine-c-v0.1.0-linux-x86-64.tar.gz")
;
; A platform with no row is not an error.  The Pi and any 32-bit target build
; from source and always will until someone publishes for them, and that is a
; deliberate decision rather than an omission: the fallback fires ONLY when this
; file declares no artifact, never when a declared one fails to fetch.
